{"slug": "7-segment-display-decoder", "targets": ["seven segment decoder", "seven segment common cathode pin configuration"], "html": "<h2 style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:700;color:#0186e0;margin:34px 0 16px;\">How to Wire a Seven Segment Decoder to a Common Cathode Display</h2><p>A <strong>seven segment decoder</strong> is a logic IC that takes a 4 bit BCD number on its inputs and switches on the correct segments a to g to show that digit. The CD4511 is the usual choice for a common cathode display because its outputs are active high and source current into each segment.</p><h3 style=\"font-size:19px;font-weight:700;color:#1a2b4a;margin:20px 0 10px;\">CD4511 to Display Pin Mapping</h3><p>The table uses the standard CD4511 16 pin DIP pinout and the seven segment common cathode pin configuration of a typical 10 pin single digit display.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Signal</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">CD4511 pin</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Display pin</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Segment a</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">13</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">7</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Segment b</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">12</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">6</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Segment c</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">11</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">4</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Segment d</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">10</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">2</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Segment e</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">9</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Segment f</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">15</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">9</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Segment g</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">14</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">10</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Common cathode</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">VSS (pin 8) and ground</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">3 and 8</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">BCD inputs A, B, C, D</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">7, 1, 2, 6</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Not used</td></tr></table></div><h3 style=\"font-size:19px;font-weight:700;color:#1a2b4a;margin:20px 0 10px;\">Wiring Steps</h3><ol><li>Connect pin 16 (VDD) to plus 5 V and pin 8 (VSS) to ground.</li><li>Tie display pins 3 and 8 to ground.</li><li>Place a 220 Ω resistor between each decoder output and its display segment pin.</li><li>Pull Lamp Test (pin 3) and Blanking (pin 4) high, and Latch Enable (pin 5) low.</li><li>Apply BCD 0000 to 1001 on inputs A to D; the digit 0 to 9 appears.</li></ol><p>Always check the datasheet of your display, as some parts use a different pinout.</p>"}